#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157135 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/id #uuid "e5b64c26-300d-530d-b22f-ff061f4e3296" :question/math-academy-id "e-7657" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4e^x - 5 >  0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{x}-5 & >0 \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & >5 \\\\ {e}^{x} & >\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157136 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "95104c90-f452-5551-adec-836ef1192276" :question/math-academy-id "e-7660" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3 \\cdot 5^x <   7^{3-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{x} & <{7}^{3-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7) & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157142 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/id #uuid "1c336514-aac7-5f9c-8118-8a16188f2d42" :question/math-academy-id "e-7703" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5 \\cdot \\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^x + 2 \\geq 12.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 10 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 3 - \\ln 4$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157143 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/id #uuid "185e4c00-f218-5d54-8345-397267a74b6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7704" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ![](images/2a/2abe9cdeada000e13f085d8be9750f26a184e68557283f1bdec4e003afabf173.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n![](images/c7/c7b01893901c216f2285f49c2fb38819dc193d003881b4e5719867f9c1863844.svg)\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since ![](images/e1/e19edf5f19279dad194a5f66ac4979df2518fedd68e0f768877ee0b5024b3540.svg) we have\n![](images/86/864dbe8402aa67932c2fcb31f22012c4904f575976d1fc62b027bb84443b6c3f.svg)\nfor every real value of ![](images/cf/cff7b257e3c71ec70dc7cd273bdd1cced4254993a43cffbfc338460c21bc7319.svg)\n\nIn particular, every value of ![](images/69/69e8d1fbd034994ec8c4d373a6428eb5f0344c0c9d71f7c624525b550fa73fdb.svg) is greater than ![](images/2e/2ed49f2115a6860b40a8ea69b5672921fb832f8592a69969e4c9f4bb370c1aa6.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/2f/2f954e20489fbbbc6b2a5e7a577ab39e8f4ea3769202c1db83c2db99549caec0.svg)\nfor all real values of ![](images/cf/cff7b257e3c71ec70dc7cd273bdd1cced4254993a43cffbfc338460c21bc7319.svg)\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018d8348-3190-5eea-b417-c63541e4aff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247430} {:answer/id #uuid "08eab5fa-3385-5df5-b8fd-2f29432c498e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247499} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb0449b-6ed5-5ab7-9e17-f2f9074c0612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247696} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1524c0-3b53-58d5-b338-ecb0ead5ac3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247832} {:answer/id #uuid "67f62f68-93fb-55cb-a9ad-f01fff119dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "018d8348-3190-5eea-b417-c63541e4aff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247430} :answer-field/id #uuid "078894b3-54fc-5c41-9c56-f018aef4c9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9725f40a-ccc5-5b84-9193-a09e8482b8d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115218" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}\\le \\frac{1}{9}.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ (x+1)(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le -\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & \\ge \\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a472223-cb8b-539b-b7c5-6ad022cd38f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548375} {:answer/id #uuid "35eb7433-2b86-503d-88e3-21340443b26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548461} {:answer/id #uuid "850770e0-edac-557e-a109-7fc5d8e886b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548613} {:answer/id #uuid "94e97f3c-853a-500e-b908-e2f7624c05ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548644} {:answer/id #uuid "b7154257-b8f9-54a3-83c0-a048530cced6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94e97f3c-853a-500e-b908-e2f7624c05ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548644} :answer-field/id #uuid "14298f8b-3948-5e99-b379-c1c2f8594591" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1b6f8d1-34e2-5323-8ed2-7acebb0e0e39" :question/math-academy-id "q-115220" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x-1}-8\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x-1}-8 & \\le 0 \\\\ 4{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x-1} & \\le 8 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x-1} & \\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x-1}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ (x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{7}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ (x-1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ad2df3d-a449-5d94-ab6c-aab6de9bf8c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548521} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfad3af-61d9-5b4e-a464-116b7a05b6d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548594} {:answer/id #uuid "a2104c2f-e950-55e6-a073-9450db1a0659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548673} {:answer/id #uuid "c89862f7-5f6e-5115-848d-958f4d4ddd5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548749} {:answer/id #uuid "db8cda54-359f-5abf-a0c6-037e42f00934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db8cda54-359f-5abf-a0c6-037e42f00934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548782} :answer-field/id #uuid "98b6ea48-7da3-5707-b0d9-eff4c7665ffd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74b59c8c-c1aa-50c4-b3ef-52e18b9395b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115225" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{3x-1}+1>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{3x-1}+1 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{3x-1} & >-1 \\\\ {7}^{3x-1} & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $7>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{7}^{3x-1}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of ${7}^{3x-1}$ is greater than $-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{7}^{3x-1}>-2\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026efd52-bc0e-598f-8510-56c0f360eb76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247441} {:answer/id #uuid "26cc08ff-87ed-524a-b566-888354bcfb6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247766} {:answer/id #uuid "60613cd8-1bd4-5bcc-9818-293168e6db7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248299} {:answer/id #uuid "abff0774-73f0-51c2-ada7-120345b1c0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248955} {:answer/id #uuid "c06fecbc-699f-5e65-a769-42ba1444c6d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abff0774-73f0-51c2-ada7-120345b1c0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248955} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e1b1c42-32ff-57b4-83ab-04ed1baf84d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ced4ffe4-e7bc-5404-a238-528823539017" :question/math-academy-id "q-115231" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4>-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4 & >-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5} & >-6 \\\\ (0.43)^{4x+5} & >-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $0.43>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n(0.43)^{4x+5}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of $(0.43)^{4x+5}$ is greater than $-4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n(0.43)^{4x+5}>-4\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ea1b4e-9b93-5d12-a61c-da5590d29688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548393} {:answer/id #uuid "318198a1-93a0-5e0e-bd4a-c9298fd3a2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548453} {:answer/id #uuid "75d0224e-d58b-5aa6-bb35-6f8ff9e81486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡0.12-\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548571} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6fab65-6235-5f96-88f7-f4a6f14f98f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.12-\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.33}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548665} {:answer/id #uuid "ee656d00-69e5-5d75-b16c-4ea445df504d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "318198a1-93a0-5e0e-bd4a-c9298fd3a2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548453} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e59730-d10e-5bda-9d7f-0932d9e92317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36396874-2543-5347-91a0-563733d2b600" :question/math-academy-id "q-115242" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-(0.33)^{4x+1}\\le (0.12)^{x-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0.12)^{x-4}$ is always positive and $-(0.33)^{4x+1}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n-(0.33)^{4x+1}<(0.12)^{x-4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "394737bc-773a-5052-8889-796adf884851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247932} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed1b089-fa72-501c-a9f2-f3ec109cf231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247979} {:answer/id #uuid "a77efff0-96b8-5338-a4e0-5761d1e89e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248912} {:answer/id #uuid "cf85cd4c-7629-5a3b-801d-506323092847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249266} {:answer/id #uuid "dad2bd11-14ed-5f74-ac33-0ded3a42bf6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "394737bc-773a-5052-8889-796adf884851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247932} :answer-field/id #uuid "d44037dc-fa0e-5905-a29e-40cda249ba9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dea1588-01f3-5e0d-9811-784ff06fa34a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115248" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${7}^{x+1}>{5}^{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{x+1} & >{5}^{x-1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{x+1}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x-1}) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & >(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & >x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x & >-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb7116a0-6991-5a7c-bb2e-44f6d98b2da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\ln 6}{2\\ln 8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb7116a0-6991-5a7c-bb2e-44f6d98b2da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\ln 6}{2\\ln 8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548859} :answer-field/id #uuid "2317ac7a-6810-5ebd-b759-0ad3059d4d54" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155258} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4d0b791-662a-50d7-9a51-777bd93033b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4d0b791-662a-50d7-9a51-777bd93033b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548844} :answer-field/id #uuid "32f00e3e-0ee2-5b85-a1c5-2b6e6779f01e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a7e522c-a79d-5bda-94b9-530843463aa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-276944" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2\\cdot {8}^{2x}<\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $8$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({8}^{2x}) & <{log}_{8}⁡(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ 2x & <{log}_{8}⁡({6}^{-1}) \\\\ 2x & <-{log}_{8}⁡(6) \\\\ x & <-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{8}⁡(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea38e6be-f014-5512-a1ba-8ccb320a90bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{4}(14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293846} {:answer/id #uuid "91970135-4657-52ee-9605-13ea624195a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{4}(14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91970135-4657-52ee-9605-13ea624195a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{4}(14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548640} :answer-field/id #uuid "12bd06f5-c128-5ece-a1ad-42f93aab1e1c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293989} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d80fb632-82ac-5a2d-82ff-8e718314f84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293771} {:answer/id #uuid "2b25051c-b8bf-5ffe-b946-a66583b2154e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b25051c-b8bf-5ffe-b946-a66583b2154e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548438} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a79b385-65a0-5ead-8571-f9e6b4e68eee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d5edd14-7e7b-53cf-8d2d-da6793a07e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-277846" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{x}>7.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{x} & >7 \\\\ {4}^{x} & >14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $4$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{x}) & >{log}_{4}⁡(14) \\\\ x & >{log}_{4}⁡(14).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36c91c58-a410-5c55-87bc-e96d25c23eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36c91c58-a410-5c55-87bc-e96d25c23eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548464} :answer-field/id #uuid "b219f5c4-1405-5df3-8409-1c09dc28bb30" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548942} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d7e613-89f8-5827-98a8-0f87820ea103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53d7e613-89f8-5827-98a8-0f87820ea103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548512} :answer-field/id #uuid "de8ff1b6-dbc5-59c7-b40e-6086f8c8b93d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c14a17a4-e51c-5a30-87f2-f52799025a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-277850" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2}}{2\\sqrt{e}}>e.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2}}{2\\sqrt{e}} & >e \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2e\\sqrt{e} \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2e\\cdot {e}^{1/2} \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2{e}^{(1+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2{e}^{3/2} \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & >3{e}^{3/2} \\\\ {e}^{x} & >{e}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3/2}) \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9f12d8b-5eba-5053-9ae2-25dc56f3092e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9f12d8b-5eba-5053-9ae2-25dc56f3092e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548779} :answer-field/id #uuid "54a07fba-ccc2-5984-811e-36401527375b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548910} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067a4aa5-e856-53d1-a787-9e23be107370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "067a4aa5-e856-53d1-a787-9e23be107370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548367} :answer-field/id #uuid "89ff50ea-6ada-5e88-ad68-fd426c6d4ccb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1731e842-6496-5676-8984-724a57e1229e" :question/math-academy-id "q-277889" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}>-(0.52)^{4x-3}.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<\\,x\\,<$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Since ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}$ is always positive and $-(0.52)^{4x-3}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}>-(0.52)^{4x-3}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-\\infty <x<\\infty \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1300c640-ecde-57fd-837d-d262fb2412e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548392} {:answer/id #uuid "49c537e9-a3cd-55e9-8199-34801bb2696a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.98)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548495} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6c3de8-6a0c-504c-945d-c2b5ae34d929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548627} {:answer/id #uuid "8edd91cd-4c33-519f-b4fc-3d1b8dbd21ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.98)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548634} {:answer/id #uuid "eb314a9b-6e16-5467-8ef7-c1e828ba21c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c6c3de8-6a0c-504c-945d-c2b5ae34d929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548627} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fc155ab-c27b-5344-bdef-c950aecc2f25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2013b040-4b7b-5305-9113-8c2d239f0c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-365305" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3e-\\frac{1}{2}{(0.99)}^{x}>-2e.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3e-\\frac{1}{2}{(0.99)}^{x} & >-2e \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{(0.99)}^{x} & >-5e \\\\ {(0.99)}^{x} & <10e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.99)^{x}] & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e8b3b6f-beec-552d-9868-ed6a0f8961df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321259} {:answer/id #uuid "1c557786-2967-59f8-9bd5-79da655905ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321362} {:answer/id #uuid "5f13a860-aaa5-50a7-bd99-bf37fedc3d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321896} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb5e32-2058-558c-8345-f421ebabdca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322111} {:answer/id #uuid "cd89b622-3090-56f6-b614-bf5d1b191223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c557786-2967-59f8-9bd5-79da655905ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321362} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec706b48-8cdc-560f-b7d1-8876a9a34e0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4215e98b-bff5-5507-9fa2-a2d9f87a2aa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-365326" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}>{3}^{2-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2} & >{3}^{2-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡[3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x-2}] & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2-3x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(x-2)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >(2-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29435c7e-2913-51cb-b1ca-bb88c5109d0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality with Exponential Functions on Both Sides"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c3cfe2-4355-5ff2-ab48-e1edb2a062fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548434} {:answer/id #uuid "30f97a9c-235e-5b9d-ad94-5f5faf7b30d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548452} {:answer/id #uuid "65ce5038-a8fa-57b9-ae79-4389ab61c39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548547} {:answer/id #uuid "975cd023-3182-5ea3-b55f-a53cbc308e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548650} {:answer/id #uuid "f340d91e-1c83-5dd3-a48f-6d49a10addf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27c3cfe2-4355-5ff2-ab48-e1edb2a062fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548434} :answer-field/id #uuid "6516c76a-3a0c-5cff-b332-8d24558288a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9ce2daa-e38d-5bb8-b99f-f6261d8e2ed1" :question/math-academy-id "q-365328" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x}\\ge {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x} & \\ge {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4}] \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\ge 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8.$ Since $3<8,$ we know that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3<\\operatorname{ln}⁡8.$ Therefore, $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8<0.$ Because we are dividing both sides of the inequality by a negative number, we must reverse the direction of the inequality sign:\n\n$$\nx\\le \\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07843e2a-4ff0-5606-88dd-fb6df01f2a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548371} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee6e0f1-6d16-57f9-853b-709deedcda1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548597} {:answer/id #uuid "99017c36-c974-535a-a6b7-1a935211faff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548657} {:answer/id #uuid "acfe2f60-036e-554b-a924-88f87430de8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548696} {:answer/id #uuid "faa1a3c0-c86e-5107-b638-b2b6168befce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99017c36-c974-535a-a6b7-1a935211faff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548657} :answer-field/id #uuid "83f053be-9090-5811-8315-530372e873fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b7ad171-3d97-5b1b-b8d8-95af987a1b58" :question/math-academy-id "q-87581" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3{e}^{x}-4<0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{x}-4 & <0 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & <4 \\\\ {e}^{x} & <\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ x & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b61ea8-195c-57d8-8a35-b7bdb9f2ff8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156317} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ded0b6-547f-510a-8787-f44bf3ea2fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156319} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae50bd1-5e8a-5b36-a5c0-79150a140e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>{log}_{5}⁡(2)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548379} {:answer/id #uuid "1d19a1e7-c62e-5dd9-8e2c-c2fd148e9c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<3-{log}_{5}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548407} {:answer/id #uuid "a72dc1b4-cf24-53eb-af85-cf883c727da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>3-{log}_{5}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96b61ea8-195c-57d8-8a35-b7bdb9f2ff8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156317} :answer-field/id #uuid "593effad-5f88-50e4-96ba-9db1dece841d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55f2f971-d19d-5674-bd56-577acd359679" :question/math-academy-id "q-87594" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $2\\cdot {5}^{3-x}+4>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {5}^{3-x}+4 & >0 \\\\ 2\\cdot {5}^{3-x} & >-4 \\\\ {5}^{3-x} & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $5>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{5}^{3-x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of ${5}^{3-x}$ is greater than $-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{5}^{3-x}>-2\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04309d45-2991-5d4b-9dc9-b75422610ab6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ce5010-9bbd-5ade-899e-fe1a3f097428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548507} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5e4a4c-1f14-58c2-b546-bbbe0317d298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548527} {:answer/id #uuid "cf312d58-edda-5e8f-ba13-2b32799659a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548761} {:answer/id #uuid "e79d4b8f-48ac-5d71-b6cb-54ae49de9a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548809} {:answer/id #uuid "f34a907c-f391-5979-9222-48eb1c76e838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e79d4b8f-48ac-5d71-b6cb-54ae49de9a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548809} :answer-field/id #uuid "41f9833e-1de8-5784-982b-34d3e897640a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3a9275d-4331-54c5-bd20-117d217d8aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-87718" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5{e}^{x}+2\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{x}+2 & \\le 4 \\\\ 5{e}^{x} & \\le 2 \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\le \\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ x & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c40639-3c77-50c8-8f1c-bbadeb578a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351873} {:answer/id #uuid "762d8584-356c-5934-bef4-eaafc9ebfb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352605} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b9eb79-14b7-5185-8a50-0fd215992e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353140} {:answer/id #uuid "db1e0f4d-4196-507d-b1ad-1cb812c5526a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353471} {:answer/id #uuid "fcda1a1b-27c7-5555-bcad-2e5b620275f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "762d8584-356c-5934-bef4-eaafc9ebfb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352605} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0f1dab6-f8cf-5d41-b492-b3aa875e7c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "653d874d-a486-580e-a2d8-67e03055d454" :question/math-academy-id "q-87828" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}<25.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <25 \\\\ {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}] & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{9}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05f94d64-fdf7-55d8-80fb-3988a47ed2d4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b1cc1bf-b9c7-5a29-aae6-fd2227f68750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548437} {:answer/id #uuid "471adf4a-2ee6-55c5-b17b-f61758f27ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548491} {:answer/id #uuid "9b511230-9e3a-54c3-89e3-32a71b42d774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548662} {:answer/id #uuid "a290065e-b826-5644-8f29-3001cc4d7b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548675} {:answer/id #uuid "b772fffb-c14a-5583-b222-fea287da527c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a290065e-b826-5644-8f29-3001cc4d7b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548675} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed31c44d-45d6-599a-8504-8f396b5ed76f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46e111c4-e425-556e-9ed0-200d0e8bd4c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-87829" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${(0.45)}^{x+3}-10\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(0.45)}^{x+3}-10 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {(0.45)}^{x+3} & \\ge 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.45)^{x+3}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ (x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23732147-0ec5-530e-8b60-fef4f9aef0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351886} {:answer/id #uuid "ae126722-fc12-5191-a374-4e58c15fb502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353044} {:answer/id #uuid "b51c2b64-41d9-519a-b633-676091e8f9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353113} {:answer/id #uuid "c113b2fd-2e34-50e6-9d87-4f24e3685f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353234} {:answer/id #uuid "c75a274e-9aa1-53dd-bf1b-0ff0ced8d750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23732147-0ec5-530e-8b60-fef4f9aef0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351886} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03eebba-6afb-5ac5-a43d-5f0738e73b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48ad6fff-2c0d-5121-b124-5aea1ae6b3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-87876" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-4-{e}^{3x}>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4-{e}^{3x} & >2 \\\\ -{e}^{3x} & >6 \\\\ {e}^{3x} & <-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $e>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nTherefore, ${e}^{3x}$ cannot be less than $-6.$ Hence, the inequality\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}<-6\n$$\n\nhas no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a114450f-bfcd-5db3-9060-fcd067c606fa" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6a91cd-ec94-5ffa-9fc6-4ea1001fa56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548380} {:answer/id #uuid "34afea07-154e-5055-aa83-267d189909a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548458} {:answer/id #uuid "606b4fd3-98e4-5ae8-9cb9-535434209a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>8-{log}_{4}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548537} {:answer/id #uuid "6225fbff-5d95-5caa-a071-92945444e0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<1+{log}_{8}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548542} {:answer/id #uuid "8a652a53-660b-549e-8508-3fead716c4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>4-{log}_{8}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34afea07-154e-5055-aa83-267d189909a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548458} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff518e7d-7337-524b-9fce-f0af17d5e2c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1c3e141-e136-55c6-9d3b-793077b3eeeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-87888" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${8}^{2-4x}+11>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2-4x}+11 & >0 \\\\ {8}^{2-4x} & >-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $8>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{8}^{2-4x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of ${8}^{2-4x}$ is greater than $-11.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{8}^{2-4x}>-11\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers."}]}
